---
description: Ban try/catch (and try/finally) in Playwright specs; push cleanup/error handling to fixtures or the API/page-object layer
globs: "tests/**/*.spec.ts"
alwaysApply: false
---

# No try/catch in Specs

Specs assert outcomes; they must not contain `try`/`catch`/`finally`. If a test needs setup, cleanup, or error handling, move it to a fixture, page object, or API client method instead.

## Rules

- Never write `try`, `catch`, or `finally` inside a `test(...)` body.
- Resource cleanup (e.g. deleting a created record) belongs in fixture teardown — the code that runs after `await use(...)`.
- If cleanup needs a value only known at runtime (e.g. a created id), have the fixture expose a tracking mechanism (an array, a registry) that the spec populates; the fixture drains it after `use(...)`.
- Expected-failure assertions (e.g. an API call that should 401/404) are not error handling — just `await` the call and assert on the response/status; do not wrap it in `try/catch`.

## Example

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — cleanup via try/finally in the spec
test('creates a user', async ({ goRestUser }) => {
  const created = await goRestUser.create(payload);
  try {
    // assertions...
  } finally {
    await goRestUser.delete(created.id);
  }
});
```

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD — fixture owns cleanup
// fixtures/api.ts
createdUserIds: async ({ goRestUser }, use) => {
  const ids: number[] = [];
  await use(ids);
  for (const id of ids) {
    await goRestUser.delete(id);
  }
},
```

```typescript
// tests/api/users.spec.ts
test('creates a user', async ({ goRestUser, createdUserIds }) => {
  const created = await goRestUser.create(payload);
  createdUserIds.push(created.id);
  // assertions...
});
```
